   205	### A10.1.1 A conditional UTC assessment — RECOMMENDED (owner and Codex), BROADEN, AWAITING AUTHOR RULING
   206	
   207	Source: the decision memo, Part 1 (U1) and Part 3; its Codex review, F03, F04 and "Questions checked" 1; the
   208	tolerance evidence file. A10.1 stays as decided whatever the author rules here.
   209	
   210	**Notation.** h* is the receipt proof's selected inclusion height (A10.5). A2.1 counts it as confirmation one, so
   211	h*+5 is confirmation six. r(j) is the real (UTC) time at which block j was first produced.
   212	
   213	**Assumption A-UTC** (`assumption_id: "A-UTC-7200-6"`). *At least one of the six blocks h* … h*+5 has a header
   214	time no more than 7200 s before it was actually mined*, that is, `nTime(j) ≥ r(j) − 7200 s` for some j in
   215	[h*, h*+5]. A-UTC is an assumption of the UTC assessment only. It is not a Bitcoin consensus rule, the verifier
   216	does not check it, and no bundle carries evidence of it. It is a sufficient one-sided assumption, not a minimal one.
   217	Δ = 7200 s and m = 6 are frozen in the governing specification's catalog, not in the mutable trust file, and are
   218	not shown to be optimal.
   219	
   220	**Recommended claim text**, added after the A10.1 claim it qualifies (rows 20–21):
   221	
   222	> Under assumption A-UTC (at least one of the blocks h* … h*+5 has a header time no more than 7200 s before it was
   223	> actually mined; an assumption of the UTC assessment, not a consensus rule, and not checked by the verifier), the
   224	> bytes and both signatures existed before U = max nTime(h* … h*+5) + 7200 s, in UTC. V1 reports U only in
   225	> `utc_assessment`, beside its assumption identifier, outside the six stable fields. A2.1's refusal to bound the
   226	> observation lag B − C is unchanged.
   227	
   228	**Derivation** (memo, checked by Codex). (1) `A_sig` existed before r(h*), because h*'s merkle root commits to
   229	`SHA256(A_sig)` under the registered commitment and history assumptions. (2) r(h*) ≤ r(j) for every j ≥ h*,
   230	because block j commits to a chain that contains h*. (3) A-UTC supplies j in [h*, h*+5] with r(j) ≤ nTime(j) + 7200.
   231	(4) So r(h*) ≤ r(j) ≤ nTime(j) + 7200 ≤ U. The chain-order claims of A10.1 rest on steps 1–2 only.
   232	
   233	**Output contract** (memo, F03). `utc_assessment` contains `assumption_id: "A-UTC-7200-6"` and `utc_upper_bound`
   234	(RFC 3339 string or null). It is computed from the receipt proof's selected h* and its six qualified headers. Its
   235	bound is conditional on A-UTC and the existing commitment/history assumptions. It is null exactly when T is null.
   236	It does not change the six-field V1 stable result (A9:162, A9.4). Reporting a value with INVALID or UNVERIFIABLE
   237	does not establish a valid service receipt or valid service signatures. U is never a stable field, and V1's
   238	documentation and relying-party text never state U without A-UTC beside it.
   239	
   240	**Arithmetic and the 2106 boundary** (memo, F04). Verification of a preserved V1 receipt does not consult the
   241	current clock. Each bundled nTime is the profile's unsigned epoch-seconds value, widened before arithmetic:
   242	`U_seconds = 7200 + max(uint64(nTime(h*)), …, uint64(nTime(h*+5)))`, encoded as an RFC 3339 UTC string without
   243	reduction modulo 2^32 (for max nTime = 4294967295, U = `2106-02-07T08:28:15Z`, not a 1970 time). This 64-bit
   244	arithmetic covers preserved headers. Timestamp representation for Bitcoin blocks produced after the 2106 rollover
   245	of the 32-bit field is a Bitcoin protocol matter outside V1; the profile's serial-time unwrapping applies to DNSSEC
   246	RRSIG fields only.
   247	
   248	**What the signature does and does not do.** If the author adopts this section, his signature adopts A-UTC as a
   249	trust assumption. It does not establish that miners satisfy it, and nothing in V1 can.
   250	
   251	**Justification of the tolerance against the intended adversary**
   252	(`docs/decisions/2026-10-17-a-utc-tolerance-evidence.md`). The intended adversary is A9's backdater (A9:40): bytes
   253	that exist only at real time e, presented with a receipt whose U is earlier than e.
   254	- **Failure condition.** A-UTC fails, and U can be wrong, only if the miners of all six confirming blocks set
   255	  lagging timestamps: every block h* … h*+5 has a header time more than 7200 s before it was mined.
   256	- **A non-mining backdater** can choose only when to stamp and which proof to present. Lowest-height selection
   257	  gives it no favourable choice. Over 559,718 observed six-block windows (heights 369,715–941,944, 2015–2026; Bitcoin
   258	  Block Arrival Time Dataset, multiple observers, CC0), every window contained a block first seen within 1,065 s of
   259	  its header time. U exceeded the inclusion block's first-seen time by at least 6,135 s (median 9,908 s). The
   260	  largest single-block lag observed was 3,918 s; the median was 18 s. A mempool.space sample of 481 blocks (2024)
   261	  and Bowden et al. (arXiv:1801.07447, 2018) are consistent with this.
   262	- **Consensus.** A header must exceed the median of the previous eleven header times (MTP). Given the preceding
   263	  header times as they were, consensus alone forced A-UTC at some block of the window in all but 545 of those
   264	  windows (0.097%).
   265	- **A backdater who controls miners** must control the timestamps of six consecutive blocks, and in most windows
   266	  earlier blocks as well, to lower MTP. A majority coalition can do this without limit (A2.1). Against that
   267	  adversary the UTC assessment does not hold; this is the stated failure condition.
   268	- **Limits of the evidence.** The dataset's own quality check rejects any arrival more than 7200 s from its header
   269	  time, so it cannot show a lag above the threshold; its distribution below the threshold has no observation
   270	  between 3,918 s and 7,200 s. Observer clock error is not separated. The data describe ordinary miner behaviour,
   271	  not a deliberate coalition.
   272	- Δ equals Bitcoin's node-local forward tolerance; that match does not validate it. m = 6 reuses the k = 6 headers
   273	  that V1 already requires and pins, so U needs no new input.
   274	
   275	**Relation to A2.1 and guarantee 2.** A2.1 refuses to bound B − C, the service's observation lag, and keeps
   276	SLA-style bounds out of the assumption set. A-UTC bounds a different quantity (production time versus header time),
   277	and U never enters a verdict, the shipping predicate or S. Guarantee 2 keeps its chain-time meaning and is not
   278	translated to UTC (row 23).
   279	
   280	**Recorded, not obligations.** RFC 3161 tokens over `SHA256(A_sig)` from at least two independently operated
   281	time-stamp authorities (U_TSA = max_i(genTime_i + a_i), memo U2) are a recorded candidate, with no V2 or later
   282	obligation. Unresolved use requirements, also recorded only: whether a court or another examiner will accept A-UTC;
   283	whether a particular use needs qualified timestamps.
   284	
