    54	## 3. Empirical header-time lag
    55	
    56	### 3.1 Primary source: the Bitcoin Block Arrival Time Dataset
    57	
    58	**[F] Source.** `bitcoin-data/block-arrival-times`, CC0 1.0, commit `1bb1c1b750dcc4633a54f01cbaaed8d789c3f4c0`
    59	(2026-03-28), https://github.com/bitcoin-data/block-arrival-times . Each row gives height, header hash and the
    60	millisecond time at which one observer node first received or connected the block. Observers include the KIT DSN
    61	Bitcoin monitoring nodes (https://www.dsn.kastel.kit.edu/bitcoin/), 0xB10C's peer-observer and monitoring nodes, and
    62	four independently operated Bitcoin Core nodes. Header times are from the dataset's
    63	`qa/block-timestamps/block-timestamps.csv` (SHA-256
    64	`85800b9110c2784f6711ebd348164f7de0a140692622251a4d8ee964a445046f`). The concatenated `data/*.csv` files hash to
    65	`d83509d9e0e22d22abce29f76ee636335051941c3fed981fe52b28531f4db90c` (`cat data/*.csv | sha256sum`).
    66	
    67	**Method.** For each height, *first-seen* is the earliest arrival across all observers. The `stratum_work_*` files
    68	are excluded, because their row for height N records the arrival of block N−1. The lag of block j is
    69	`first-seen(j) − nTime(j)`. A block is first seen after it is produced, so this lag is **an upper bound on
    70	r(j) − nTime(j)**, the quantity A-UTC bounds, provided the observer clock is accurate. Computed 2026-10-17 by the
    71	script in section 6.
    72	
    73	**[F] Coverage.** 559,831 heights from 369,715 (2015-08-13) to 941,944 (2026-03-23), 97.8% of that range.
    74	
    75	**[F] Lag of single blocks** (first-seen minus nTime, seconds):
    76	
    77	| Statistic | Value |
    78	|---|---|
    79	| median | 18.1 |
    80	| 25th / 75th percentile | 9.0 / 29.0 |
    81	| 95th / 99th / 99.9th percentile | 48.4 / 80.0 / 237.0 |
    82	| maximum | 3,917.6 (height 409,854, 2016) |
    83	| blocks above 600 s / 1,800 s / 3,600 s / 7,200 s | 93 / 20 / 5 / 0 |
    84	| blocks below 0 s (header time after first-seen) | 34,461 (6.2%); minimum −4,417.8 |
    85	
    86	**[F] Six-block windows** (559,718 windows h … h+5 with all six heights covered):
    87	
    88	| Statistic | Value |
    89	|---|---|
    90	| largest "smallest lag in the window" | 1,064.9 s |
    91	| 99.9th percentile of "smallest lag in the window" | 30.1 s |
    92	| U minus first-seen(h) (U computed as max nTime(h … h+5) + 7200) | minimum 6,135.1 s; median 9,908.3 s |
    93	| X − 7200 = max nTime(h … h+5) − nTime(h) | median 2,727 s; 99th percentile 6,899 s |
    94	
    95	**[E] Interpretation.** In every observed window, at least one block's header time was within 1,065 s of its
    96	first-seen time, so A-UTC held with at least 6,135 s to spare. No observed single block, in any window, came within
    97	3,282 s of the 7200 s tolerance. In this sample, a non-mining backdater who stamps at a chosen time never met a
    98	window that violated A-UTC. The typical margin of U over the inclusion block's first-seen time is about 2.75 h.
    99	
   100	**[F] Limits of this source.**
   101	- **Censoring at the threshold.** The dataset's CI (`qa/block-timestamps/check-block-timestamps.py`) fails if any
   102	  arrival differs from its header time by more than 7200 s ("The listed timestamps might have been recorded during
   103	  IBD or the system clock was wrong?"). The dataset therefore cannot contain a lag above 7200 s, whatever happened.
   104	  Its zero count above 7200 s is not evidence. The distribution below the threshold is not censored: there is no
   105	  observation between 3,918 s and 7,200 s.
   106	- **Observer clocks.** Lag includes propagation, observer delay and observer clock error. A fast observer clock
   107	  inflates the lag (conservative); a slow one understates it. The negative lags (6.2% of blocks) show miner clock
   108	  error, future-dated headers or observer clock error. The dataset does not separate them.
   109	- **Missing heights.** Windows with a missing height (2.2% of heights) are excluded. If rows were removed for
   110	  exceeding the CI threshold, the removal would hide exactly the windows of interest.
   111	- **History, not adversary.** The sample shows ordinary miner behaviour from 2015 to 2026. It does not bound a
   112	  coalition that sets lagging timestamps on purpose.
   113	
   114	### 3.2 Corroborating sources
   115	
   116	- **[F] mempool.space Block Time Report** (https://research.mempool.space/p/09defe3d-c836-44e7-b244-7f8434e72151).
   117	  One observer, arrival over the mempool.space websocket versus header time, 481 blocks between heights 857,514
   118	  and 858,080 (2024-08-19 to 2024-08-23): minimum −69 s, maximum +82 s, median 41 s, mean 40 s. The author describes
   119	  it as "quick and dirty" and attributes the negative values to drifted clocks at some pools (Braiins, F2Pool,
   120	  Foundry USA, Luxor, MARA Pool). Observer arrival, not production time.
   121	- **[F] Bowden, Keeler, Krzesinski and Taylor, "Block arrivals in the Bitcoin blockchain", arXiv:1801.07447
   122	  (2018), §II-B.** "Of the 500,000 blocks mined up to November 2017, 13,618 of them have a timestamp prior to that
   123	  of the previous block and some 1,000 of them have timestamps more than 10 minutes prior to the previous block
   124	  timestamp"; "Some inter-arrival times are more negative than −7,000 seconds but most are between −1,500 and 0
   125	  seconds." The authors attribute out-of-order times largely to future-dated headers followed by correct ones, and
   126	  to miners using the timestamp as an extra nonce. Our count from the dataset's header list over the same range is
   127	  13,638; the source of the 20-block difference is not established.
   128	- **[F] Chain-wide header order** (dataset header list, heights 1–942,357). 15,410 headers have nTime earlier than
   129	  their predecessor's. The largest backward step is 7,125 s. Since height 700,000 (2021-09): 1,040 headers, largest
   130	  backward step 2,032 s. A backward step measures disagreement between header times, not lag behind real time.
   131	
   132	## 4. What consensus forces
   133	
   134	**[F]** A header is invalid unless `nTime > MTP`, the median of the previous 11 header times (Bitcoin Core
   135	`validation.cpp`, "time-too-old"). So the largest lag a miner of block j can choose is less than r(j) − MTP(j). If
   136	r(j) − MTP(j) ≤ 7200 s, block j satisfies A-UTC whatever its miner does.
   137	
   138	**[F] Measured** with first-seen as an upper bound on r(j), over the same 559,831 blocks: first-seen(j) − MTP(j)
   139	has median 3,328 s, 99th percentile 7,824 s and maximum 21,779 s; it exceeds 7200 s for 1.9% of blocks. In only
   140	**545 of 559,718** six-block windows (0.097%) did it exceed 7200 s at all six blocks.
   141	
   142	**[E] Interpretation.** Given the header times that the preceding blocks actually had, consensus alone forced A-UTC
   143	in 99.9% of observed windows, whatever the six miners did. This is not a bound against a coalition: a coalition that
   144	also mines some of the preceding blocks, or the earlier blocks of the window, lowers the later MTPs. It shows that
   145	violating A-UTC needs control of timestamps beyond the six blocks in most windows, or a slow stretch of production.
   146	
   147	## 5. Conclusion and what remains assumed
   148	
   149	- **[R]** Δ = 7200 s and m = 6 are a defensible tolerance against a non-mining backdater. Over ten years of
   150	  observed windows, the margin under A-UTC was never less than 6,135 s. Against a backdater who controls the
   151	  timestamps of six consecutive blocks, A-UTC can fail. This is the stated failure condition, and the claim does not
   152	  hold against that adversary.
   153	- **[A]** The author's signature on A10, if it adopts §A10.1.1, adopts A-UTC as a trust assumption of the UTC
   154	  assessment. It does not establish that miners satisfy it. The verifier cannot check it; the bundle carries no
   155	  evidence of production times.
   156	- **Not decided here, recorded only:** whether a court or another examiner will accept A-UTC; whether a particular
   157	  use needs qualified (eIDAS) timestamps. RFC 3161 tokens over `SHA256(A_sig)` are a recorded candidate, not an
   158	  obligation (memo, U2).
   159	
   160	## 6. Reproduction
   161	
   162	Clone the dataset at the commit above into a fresh directory, keep the script elsewhere, and run
   163	`python3 -I lag.py <repo>`. The script:
   164	
   165	```python
   166	import csv, sys, os, glob, statistics
   167	repo = sys.argv[1]
   168	nt = {int(h): int(t) for h, t in csv.reader(open(os.path.join(repo, 'qa/block-timestamps/block-timestamps.csv')))}
   169	first = {}                                   # height -> earliest arrival (ms) across observers
   170	for p in glob.glob(os.path.join(repo, 'data/*.csv')):
   171	    if os.path.basename(p).startswith('stratum'): continue
   172	    for r in csv.reader(open(p)):
   173	        h, ms = int(r[0]), int(r[2])
   174	        if h in nt and (h not in first or ms < first[h]): first[h] = ms
   175	lag = {h: first[h] / 1000 - nt[h] for h in first}
   176	mtp = lambda h: sorted(nt[h - i] for i in range(1, 12))[5]
   177	room = {h: first[h] / 1000 - mtp(h) for h in first}
   178	wins = [h for h in lag if all(h + i in lag for i in range(6))]
   179	win_min = [min(lag[h + i] for i in range(6)) for h in wins]
   180	margin = [max(nt[h + i] for i in range(6)) + 7200 - first[h] / 1000 for h in wins]
   181	all_room = sum(1 for h in wins if all(room[h + i] > 7200 for i in range(6)))
   182	print(len(lag), statistics.median(lag.values()), max(lag.values()), max(win_min), min(margin), all_room, len(wins))
   183	```
     1	# Tessera plain-language style (inspired by ASD-STE100)
     2	
     3	Owner instance, 2026-10-16. The author asked for documentation "written so someone whose native language isn't
     4	Claude can understand it". These rules adapt the main writing rules of ASD-STE100 (Simplified Technical English).
     5	We do not claim STE conformance: we have no approved STE dictionary check. We use the structure rules and our own
     6	dictionary.
     7	
     8	Revised 2026-10-16 after the faithfulness review of the A9 pilot (`docs/reviews/plain-pilot/CODEX-REVIEW.md`,
     9	"Proposed dictionary work"). The revision adds the entries that the review lists and repairs receipt, anchor time,
    10	window, authority record, key, AWS key, VALID and INVALID. It replaces "Google key" with "second key". It removes
    11	"receipt ID", because its object was not defined. These entries are owner proposals. A reviewer must check each
    12	new entry against its registered source before publication.
    13	
    14	## Rules
    15	
    16	1. One sentence gives one idea.
    17	2. A sentence has a maximum of 20 words if it tells the reader to do something, and 25 words if it describes something.
    18	3. A paragraph has a maximum of 6 sentences and one topic. A fragment such as a list item counts as a sentence.
    19	4. Use the active voice. Say who or what does the action.
    20	5. Use only the words in the dictionary below for project ideas. Each word has one meaning. Do not use a synonym.
    21	6. Use technical names (for example "DNSSEC", "Bitcoin", "SHA-256") without change. Explain each one when you first use it.
    22	7. Do not use idioms, metaphors or humour.
    23	8. Write steps and conditions as numbered or bulleted lists.
    24	9. Write "must" for a rule and "can" for a possibility. Do not use "should", "may" or "shall".
    25	10. Do not refer to a document section number in the body. Put references at the end.
    26	
    27	## Faithfulness
    28	
    29	A plain document does not replace the signed or governing text. If the two differ, the governing text applies, and
    30	the difference is a finding. Before publication, a reviewer checks each plain document against its source. The
    31	reviewer confirms three things: the plain document contains no new rule, it removes no rule, and it changes no
    32	meaning. The reviewer records each place where the source was unclear or contradictory.
    33	
    34	A plain document does not resolve an unclear source. It renders the source as written and adds a visible marker,
    35	for example "[A9 is unclear here: see A10 draft, item A01.]". The marker names the item that must resolve it.
    36	
    37	## Dictionary
    38	
    39	Code-style names (for example `A_sig`, `NOT_EVALUATED`) are technical identifiers. Use them without change and
    40	explain them at first use.
    41	
    42	### Content, receipts and the service
    43	
    44	| Word | Meaning | Do not use |
    45	|---|---|---|
    46	| content | The bytes that a customer gives to the service. | data, document, payload |
    47	| receipt | The set of files that the service gives back for one content. A receipt is the evidence. | attestation (alone), certificate, bundle |
    48	| service | The Tessera V1 program that makes receipts. | Tessera (alone), issuer |
    49	| issuance event | One occasion on which the service makes a receipt for content. | issuance (alone) |
    50	| issue time | The time that the service writes into the frames when it signs. The verifier cannot check this time directly. | declared time |
    51	| holder | The person who keeps a receipt. | custodian |
    52	| user | The person who uses a verifier to make a decision about a receipt. | relying party (in plain text) |
    53	| attacker | A person who tries to make a false receipt, to change a true receipt, or to make a false claim about a receipt. | adversary |
    54	| operational sphere | Everything that the author uses to operate the service: the accounts, devices, credentials and signing capability. | — |
    55	| registered | Written in the signed amendments to the Tessera pre-registration. | — |
    56	
    57	### Keys and signatures
    58	
    59	| Word | Meaning | Do not use |
    60	|---|---|---|
    61	| key | A private signing key of the service. V1 has two keys, one in each slot. | signer |
    62	| slot | The named position of one service key in a receipt. V1 has two slots, `kms` and `gpg`. | — |
    63	| AWS key | The service key in the `kms` slot. It is in a hardware security module in the service's AWS account. The registered branch sets its signature algorithm. | kms key |
    64	| second key | The service key in the `gpg` slot. It is outside the AWS account, in non-exportable hardware. The dated custody record names that hardware. It uses ECDSA P-256. | Google key, gpg key, token |
    65	| key fingerprint | A short value that the verifier computes from a public key. It identifies that key. | key ID |
    66	| repository key | The key that signs the git commits that hold the git authority record. It is not a service key. | — |
    67	| DNS root key | A key of the DNS root zone that the trust file lists. DNSSEC checks start from it. | KSK (in plain text) |
    68	| release key | The key that authenticates each verifier release and its trust file. | — |
    69	| signature | The result when a key signs bytes. | — |
    70	| signature algorithm | The method that a key uses to make signatures and that a verifier uses to check them. | cipher |
    71	| signing capability | The ability to make a key sign. A person can have this ability without a copy of the private key. | key possession |
    72	| non-exportable | A property of a key in hardware: nobody can copy the private key out of the hardware. It does not stop use of the key. | — |
    73	| authority-record credentials | The credentials that let a person change an authority record: the DNS credential and the repository key. | channel credentials |
    74	
    75	### What the keys sign
    76	
    77	| Word | Meaning | Do not use |
    78	|---|---|---|
    79	| bind | Bytes bind a value when they fix that exact value, directly or through its SHA-256 hash. | — |
    80	| frame | The exact bytes that one service key signs. Each frame binds the content and the common metadata, and names the key fingerprint for its slot. | signed part, payload |
    81	| common metadata | The fields that both frames must contain with the same values. They include the domain name of the service. | — |
    82	| `A_sig` | The bytes that hold both frames and their exact signatures in a fixed format. | signed core |
    83	| receipt anchor | The SHA-256 hash of `A_sig`. OpenTimestamps puts this hash into Bitcoin. | — |
    84	
    85	### Authority
    86	
    87	| Word | Meaning | Do not use |
    88	|---|---|---|
    89	| authority record | A record that says which two keys belong to wamason.com and states their window. V1 has two authority records: one in DNS and one in a git commit. | key publication, channel |
    90	| window | The period that the authority records state for the two keys. It has a start and an end. The verifier compares the issue time with the start and T with the end. It makes no other comparison with the window. | lifetime, validity |
    91	| evidence time | The time at which the verifier checks one authority record. It is the chain time of that record's own selected block. | — |
    92	| DNS snapshot | A saved copy of the DNS records and DNSSEC signatures for the DNS authority record. The service anchors it in Bitcoin. | — |
    93	| DNS owner name | The complete DNS name that holds a DNS record. | — |
    94	| commit | A git object that records a version of the files in a git repository. A key can sign a commit. | — |
    95	| tree | A git object that lists the files and folders of one version. | — |
    96	| blob | A git object that holds the bytes of one file. | — |
    97	| object ID | The SHA-1 hash that git uses to identify a git object. | commit hash |
    98	
    99	### Bitcoin and time
   100	
   101	| Word | Meaning | Do not use |
   102	|---|---|---|
   103	| Bitcoin block | One block of the Bitcoin chain. Each block holds hashes and a header. | — |
   104	| block number | The position of a block in the Bitcoin chain. The first block has number 0. | height (in plain text) |
   105	| header | The part of a Bitcoin block that identifies the block and states its time. | — |
   106	| chain time | The time that a block header states. It is a value from Bitcoin, not a measurement of real time. | block time |
   107	| confirmation | The block that contains an anchor gives the first confirmation. Each later block gives one more confirmation. | — |
   108	| reorganization | A change in the Bitcoin chain that removes recent blocks and puts other blocks in their place. | reorg |
   109	| OpenTimestamps proof | A file that connects a hash to one or more Bitcoin blocks. | OTS proof (in plain text), timestamp |
   110	| Bitcoin attestation | A statement in an OpenTimestamps proof that a Bitcoin block at a stated block number holds a hash. It is not a receipt. | attestation (alone) |
   111	| proof path | The steps in an OpenTimestamps proof from the hash to one Bitcoin attestation. | branch |
   112	| selected block | The block of the Bitcoin attestation that the verifier selects from an OpenTimestamps proof. | — |
   113	| anchor time (T) | The chain time of the selected block for the receipt anchor. T is a chain time, not real time. | timestamp |
   114	| pin | An entry in the trust file that gives the exact header for one block number. | checkpoint |
   115	
   116	### The verifier and its results
   117	
   118	| Word | Meaning | Do not use |
   119	|---|---|---|
   120	| verifier | The program that a person uses to check a receipt. | validator |
   121	| trust file | The file that comes with a verifier. It lists the DNS root keys, the repository key and the pins. | trust configuration |
   122	| verdict | The result of a check: VALID, INVALID or CANNOT VERIFY. | status, outcome |
   123	| VALID | All of V1's registered checks passed. This does not mean that V1 checked revocation, historical security, standing, the sender or the truth of the content. | VALID_STRICT (in plain text) |
   124	| INVALID | A registered check failed. INVALID does not show that the content is false. | — |
   125	| CANNOT VERIFY | The verifier does not have what it needs to decide. This is not the same as INVALID. | UNVERIFIABLE (in plain text) |
   126	| cryptographic assumption | A registered condition that V1 does not check: the hash functions and signature algorithms are not broken. | — |
   127	| key-use assumption | A registered condition that V1 does not check: each key signs only the bytes that its rules permit. | key rules |
   128	| Bitcoin history assumption | A registered condition that V1 does not check: the Bitcoin chain history stays intact and available. | — |
   129	| revocation | A statement that a key must no longer be trusted. | — |
   130	| historical security | Whether a signature algorithm or key was still safe at the time when it signed. | historical safety |
   131	| status capture | Evidence about the revocation status of a key, saved after the anchor. V1 does not require it. | — |
   132	| standing | Evidence of which result the service gave to an attempt: the shipped receipt, or an attempt that it replaced, refused or did not ship. | — |
