    32	## Part 1. The UTC upper bound
    33	
    34	### What Bitcoin consensus does and does not bound
    35	
    36	- **[F] Median-time-past (consensus).** A header is invalid if `nTime ≤ MTP(prev)`. MTP is the median of the
    37	  previous 11 timestamps (Bitcoin Core `validation.cpp`, "time-too-old"; `chain.h`, `nMedianTimeSpan = 11`).
    38	  - This bounds a timestamp below, relative to earlier timestamps only.
    39	  - It does not bound a timestamp relative to real time. A miner may set any `nTime > MTP`, however late the block
    40	    is actually mined.
    41	- **[F] Two-hour future rule (node-local, not strict consensus).** A node rejects a header with
    42	  `nTime > now + 7200 s` (`MAX_FUTURE_BLOCK_TIME = 2*60*60`, result `BLOCK_TIME_FUTURE`). The node can accept the
    43	  block later.
    44	  - Since Bitcoin Core 27.0, `now` is the receiving node's own system clock, not network-adjusted time (PR #28956).
    45	  - The rule bounds nTime above the receiving node's clock at acceptance. If that clock is accurate to UTC, it
    46	    implies acceptance no earlier than nTime − 2 h in UTC; otherwise its clock-error bound must also be included.
    47	  - It supplies no real-time upper bound.
    48	- **[F] Timewarp (BIP 54).** BIP 54 (specification status Complete) proposes period-start nTime ≥ previous
    49	  nTime − 7200 s, and an additional period-end timestamp restriction.
    50	  - It is not active on mainnet.
    51	  - Open Core PR #35793 proposes implementing it with regtest-only activation.
    52	  - These restrictions are header-relative and supply no UTC upper bound.
    53	- **[F] Consequence (already registered, A2.1).** Consensus gives no finite bound on how far a header time may lag
    54	  real time. A coalition with a majority of hash power can keep timestamps behind real time without limit, because
    55	  MTP only has to creep upward.
    56	- **[F] Context only.** mempool.space compared arrival at one observer with header time over 481 blocks (heights
    57	  857514–858080): −69 s to +82 s, median 41 s. That is observer arrival, not production time, and not a bound.
    58	
    59	### U1: an explicit registered assumption on header times
    60	
    61	**Notation.** h* is the selected inclusion height (A10.5). It counts as confirmation one, so h*+5 is confirmation
    62	six. r(j) is the real (UTC) time at which block j was first produced.
    63	
    64	**Derivation:**
    65	1. **[F under registered assumptions]** `A_sig` existed before r(h*). The merkle root of block h* commits to
    66	   `SHA256(A_sig)` through the OTS path, under the SHA-256, OTS and history assumptions in A9.
    67	2. **[F under the hash assumption]** For every j ≥ h*, r(h*) ≤ r(j). Block j commits to the hash of a chain that
    68	   contains h*.
    69	3. **[A] A-UTC (Δ = 7200 s, m = 6).** Among blocks h* … h*+5, at least one satisfies `nTime(j) ≥ r(j) − Δ`. That is,
    70	   its timestamp is no more than two hours earlier than its production.
    71	4. For that block j: r(h*) ≤ r(j) ≤ nTime(j) + Δ ≤ max over i in [h*, h*+5] of nTime(i) + Δ.
    72	
    73	**Result.** `A_sig`, and therefore the bytes and both signatures, existed before
    74	**U = max nTime(h* … h*+5) + 7200 s**.
    75	- In the requested form, U = T + X, with **X = 7200 s + (max nTime(h* … h*+5) − T)**.
    76	- The second term comes from the receipt. With honest blocks it is typically about 50 minutes.
    77	- The stronger assumption "h* itself is honest" would give X = 7200 s exactly. **[R]** Do not adopt it: it rests
    78	  the whole claim on one miner.
    79	
    80	**What is consensus and what is assumption.**
    81	- Steps 1–2 rest on cryptographic and chain assumptions that are already registered.
    82	- Step 3 is a new assumption of the UTC assessment. It is not consensus, and the bundle cannot check it.
    83	  - A-UTC is a sufficient one-sided assumption, not a uniquely minimal one: `max nTime + 7200 ≥ r(h*)` would
    84	    itself suffice.
    85	  - m = 6 and Δ = 7200 s are not shown to be optimal. Δ is an author-selected tolerance; matching Bitcoin's
    86	    forward tolerance does not validate it. Both are frozen in the governing specification, not in the mutable
    87	    trust file.
    88	
    89	**Context [E].** If a backdating coalition holds a share α of independently found blocks, all six are theirs with
    90	probability α⁶ (7×10⁻⁴ at α = 0.3; 1.6% at α = 0.5). That is a fixed six-block experiment, not a security
    91	probability against an adversary who chooses when to submit, retries, withholds blocks or selects among proofs.
    92	
    93	**Relation to A2.1 (no conflict).** A2.1 refuses to bound B − C, the service's observation lag, and keeps
    94	SLA-style bounds out of the assumption set. A-UTC bounds a different quantity (production versus header time), and
    95	U never enters a verdict, the shipping predicate or S. The chain-order claims stand without A-UTC.
    96	
