   115	- RFC 5816 adds ESSCertIDv2, which binds the signing certificate with SHA-256.
   116	
   117	**[A] Trust.**
   118	- A single token supports "existed before genTime + accuracy". That holds only if the TSA's clock and signing
   119	  authority were trustworthy when this token was produced.
   120	- Later compromise requires evidence that distinguishes preserved historical tokens from later fabrications.
   121	- **Combining several TSAs:** for every required token i, determine a finite authenticated accuracy allowance a_i,
   122	  from the token or its pinned policy.
   123	  - Report U_TSA = max_i(genTime_i + a_i), rounded upward if the report has coarser precision.
   124	  - Under the commitment assumptions, this bounds existence if at least one required TSA's clock and signing
   125	    authority were trustworthy for its token.
   126	  - A token with no established finite allowance cannot supply this finite bound.
   127	  - Taking the minimum requires stronger trust assumptions.
   128	- **[F] Endpoint names do not show independence.** On 2026-10-17, `timestamp.entrust.net` returned a token signed by
   129	  *Sectigo* Public Time Stamping Signer R37.
   130	
   131	**[F] Long-term verifiability.**
   132	- Under the CA/B Forum Code Signing BRs (v3.11.0, §6.3.2), a TSA certificate is valid for at most 135 months, and
   133	  its key may be used for signing for at most 15 months. These are two different lifetimes.
   134	- **Pinning an intermediate CA** is a reasonable candidate. The intermediates observed on 2026-10-17 run to
   135	  2038–2041.
   136	  - It authorizes that CA's relevant issuance and moves rotation management up the chain.
   137	  - It is therefore a trust choice, not merely a maintenance optimization.
   138	- **Historical path verification:** certificate-path verification can evaluate validity at genTime using archived
   139	  certificates.
   140	  - This alone establishes neither historical TSA key safety nor revocation status.
   141	  - If revocation and historical safety are not evaluated, report those omissions separately. A9.4 concerns the
   142	    service receipt, not a new TSA trust policy.
   143	- **Independent commitment to the token:** this can preserve the token against later signature forgery.
   144	  - It is conditional on continuing commitment and history security, and on evidence that the commitment predates
   145	    the relevant compromise or algorithm cutoff.
   146	  - It does not authenticate a genTime that was already false.
   147	  - Bitcoin anchoring is one available preservation mechanism. It is neither required by RFC 3161 nor a complete
   148	    historical-trust assessment.
   149	- The TSAs sampled here all used RSA keys. That describes this sample only; the requirements permit other key
   150	  types.
   151	
   152	**[F] Free endpoints answering from this host on 2026-10-17** (one SHA-256 query each; probe output not
   153	committed): DigiCert `timestamp.digicert.com` 0.13 s; Sectigo `timestamp.sectigo.com` 0.46 s; GlobalSign
   154	`…/tsa/r6advanced1` 0.57 s (accuracy 1 s); Certum `time.certum.pl` 0.66 s (accuracy 1 s); FreeTSA timed out at
   155	20 s. Sectigo asks scripts to wait ≥ 15 s between requests (about 4 receipts per minute per client). Terms of use
   156	were not verified. These are code-signing TSAs; eIDAS *qualified* stamps (Art. 41(2), presumption of accuracy) are
   157	generally paid.
   158	
   159	**[E] Changes U2 would need:** a token bundle member, a §15.5 TSA pin list, a CMS/DER parser (ESSCertIDv2, EKU,
   160	policy, accuracy, imprint), about eight reason codes with §15.7 rows, a §4 issue-order step, a dependency-manifest
   161	entry, vectors, and both differential implementations. About 6–9 owner-days, plus preservation work; estimates, not
   162	measured. It does not fit before 30 Oct beside the 21 Oct hybrid and §14.2's open items.
   163	
   164	### U3: both
   165	
   166	The strongest option (two independent kinds of evidence), at U2's cost; not feasible before 30 Oct.
   167	
   168	### Recommendation [R]: U1 for V1; record U2 as a candidate, not an obligation
   169	
   170	1. **Trust.** U2 adds trust in the TSA's clock, certification and key use. For the long-horizon preservation
   171	   candidate, keeping independent evidence of the token before later compromise is additional work. U1 instead adds
   172	   the unverified A-UTC assumption to the existing commitment and history assumptions.
   173	2. **Schedule.** U1 costs about 1 day; U2 costs 6–9 days and would displace freeze work.
   174	3. **Record U2 as a candidate.** Use the design above: U_TSA over at least two independently operated TSAs, with
   175	   pinned intermediates and independent preservation. It applies if a use turns out to need conventional or
   176	   qualified timestamps. Do not register it as a V2 obligation (Codex point 4).
   177	4. **Unresolved use requirements, recorded but not made V1 obligations:**
   178	   - whether a court or another examiner will accept A-UTC;
   179	   - whether a particular use needs qualified timestamps.
   180	
   181	## Part 2. Capture visibility
   182	
   183	### The proposal, made exact
   184	
   185	**[F] Today.** A captor of both channel credentials (the DNS credential, and the repository key that the trust
   186	configuration pins) can publish a key assertion naming its own keys. V1 then accepts receipts under those keys
   187	(A10.4).
   188	
   189	**Mechanism.** §15.5 gains `service_keys`, an array of `{domain, kms_fp, gpg_fp, not_before, not_after, sources}`
   190	entries.
   191	- Endpoints are finite, with not_before ≤ not_after.
   192	- Fingerprints are canonical; entries are unique; the array has an explicit sort order.
   193	
   194	The new authority check is an ordered classifier, by analogy with `HEADER_CONFLICTS_PIN` versus
   195	`HEADERS_NOT_COVERED`:
   196	
   197	> First, an exact match on D, both canonical fingerprints and both inclusive window endpoints passes this check.
   198	> Otherwise, overlap with any listed window **or** not_before ≤ as_of yields INVALID / SERVICE_KEYS_NOT_LISTED.
   199	> Otherwise yield UNVERIFIABLE / TRUST_HORIZON. Overlap includes a shared endpoint. Overall verdict and reason use
   200	> the registered failure precedence, not an early return from this check.
   201	
   202	**Completeness as authorization closure:**
   203	
   204	> The initial release independently authorizes and records the complete service-key schedule through as_of. Later
   205	> releases retain every existing entry unchanged, introduce no overlapping interval, and add an entry only if its
   206	> not_before is strictly greater than the previous as_of. Thus absence through a past horizon is permanently
   207	> closed. Completeness concerns authorized entries, not arbitrary channel assertions. The release authority must
   208	> not authorize a new service-key tuple solely because the two potentially captured channels assert it.
   209	
   210	**Issuer invariant.** No receipt ships under keys that are not listed in a published release.

