   303	  `"sha1"`. It is an evidence descriptor, not a new signed-frame or key-publication field. A missing descriptor gives
   304	  `UNVERIFIABLE / GIT_OBJECT_FORMAT_MISSING`; an unsupported value gives `UNVERIFIABLE / GIT_OBJECT_FORMAT`. The
   305	  verifier recomputes the SHA-1 object ids from the raw commit/tree/blob bytes, so this descriptor cannot override
   306	  their interpretation. For V1 the stamped message is exactly 40 lowercase ASCII hex digits naming the signed commit
   307	  id followed by one LF byte. The schema/check specification and conformance cases are fixed before the 30 Oct
   308	  profile freeze; no P8 frame or key-publication vector changes are required.
   309	- **Root-KSK rule.** The root DNSKEY set is valid at the snapshot's anchored time **if some trusted KSK whose reliance
   310	  period contains that time validates it**. Each KSK entry records signing-start, signing-end and a sourced reliance
   311	  lifetime L, with the reliance interval [signing-start, signing-end + L]. L is the registered acceptance-policy
   312	  value derived from the cited root DNSKEY RRSIG validity evidence; a largest observed lifetime is labelled observed,
   313	  not asserted to be a proven global maximum. A candidate must also satisfy cryptographic validation and the selected
   314	  RRSIG's inception/expiration checks at the snapshot's own verified anchored time. This extension permits authentic
   315	  pre-retirement evidence anchored later; it does not establish that signing occurred before retirement. A thief of
